{"story":"In the valley of the Woven River, prosperity flowed from a simple creed: honor reciprocal obligations, return gifts, and punish betrayal. The people lived in round, sun-warmed houses built around the town square, where a tall cedar stood as the Grove of Return. Each year, families gave to the Grove what they could: jars of honey, woven cloth, seeds, and stories. In return, the Grove whispered blessings, turning outbound generosity into inward plenty. When a harvest waned, the town would recall the debts—who owed whom, who had promised, who had failed—and the people would mend the threads of obligation until the loom of life sang again.

The most sacred rite was the Exchange Night. Lanterns floated along the river, each gift tethered to a memory. A grandmother might return the song her mother had given, a merchant would repay a debt with a new loom, a child would gift a memory to the town for safe keeping. The reciprocity kept the river from drying and the granaries from emptiness.

But betrayal stirred the Grove’s wrath. A merchant who took without returning found his name rubbed from the Ledger of Return, the lanterns dimmed, and misfortune shadowed his door until restitution was made. Thus the community prospered only as long as its promises were kept, and never, never betrayed."}

